## Option 1 — Board-ready: "The Reversibility Register"

### Thesis (one sentence)

The most valuable thing DFW can produce before Terminal F's remaining modules release for fabrication is not a design change but a governance artifact: a module-by-module register of what is irreversible, what stays cheap to defer, and which specific options — each with a named owner, cost order of magnitude, and installation window — the airport should buy now.

### Communication job

By the end, the DFW Board and CEO's office should direct staff to produce and adopt a Terminal F reversibility register within one board cycle, because the decisions that cap 2043 capacity are being locked module-by-module on fabrication schedules the board has never seen as a decision list.

### Opening scene

August 2026, overnight, the north end of the DFW terminal area: a 3,320-ton module — 278 feet by 136 feet — creeps across the airfield on transporters and is set down within three-quarters of an inch of survey (`airport-coo::ev-f750431bb93f`). The scene is chosen for what it proves: by the time a module makes that trip, every structural, MEP, and geometric decision inside it was locked months earlier, in a factory. The building's real decision calendar is invisible from the board room.

### Argument sequence (six beats)

1. **The frame:** Terminal F went from $1.6B/15 gates to $4B/31 gates in May 2025, with the U&L extended to 2043 — a twenty-year single-carrier commitment (`infrastructure-economist::ev-9df5b63ec5a6`, `airline-commercial-strategist::ev-d7a668bca9ed`, `airline-commercial-strategist::ev-854a1201ffed`).
2. **The correction:** the operative deadline is release-for-fabrication per module, weeks to months before concrete-cure; post-fabrication modular is *more* change-hostile than stick-built (Chief Engineer and Quantitative Analyst briefs; evidence-map §4 Tension E; `airport-coo::ev-f750431bb93f`). The 30% savings claim is DFW-supplied and unaudited (`infrastructure-economist::ev-56a69208d46e`, `contrarian::ev-3cd6d9a7b69b`) — the savings are not the story; the accelerated design-lock is.
3. **The pour-locked list:** FIS floor plate and sterile-corridor geometry (`chief-engineer::ev-7dd287447303`, `virtual-chris::ev-394191fe6e5a`), apron column-and-jetway grid and MARS capability (records 80–81, 203, 251), the Skylink node (`airport-coo::ev-9b8b1fc6f240`), column grid, baggage tunnel, hydrant loop, electrical service (evidence-map §7).
4. **The cheap-to-defer list:** lounge fit-out, casework, checkpoint equipment, gate technology — provided risers, grease-waste rough-ins, conduit, PoE++ budget, and structural allowances ride into the factory drawings now (evidence-map §3; Tech Scout records 77–79, 122–124, treated as directional per §9).
5. **The named options and their price:** a Flagship-convertible lounge shell (25,000–40,000 sf allowance) versus the Quantitative Analyst's arithmetic that building the lounge itself displaces 1.6–4.0 gates, $207–600M in foregone gate value; MARS at a defined minority of stands; FIS sized to CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth against AA's international shift toward DFW (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-6f157cc0ecd1`, `airline-commercial-strategist::ev-5b76a2fb6b0f`).
6. **The governance close:** at 82.6% of passengers, AA holds effective single-carrier veto under the MII architecture (record cluster 30/68/93; thresholds confidential, per §9) — so every option must be either contractually pre-approved with rate treatment or housed outside the exclusive-use envelope. The register is where design and contract meet. Eight of twelve agents converged here independently (evidence-map §3).

### Strongest objection and where it enters

The Contrarian's option-premium discipline — "options accumulate into a shopping list no future budget funds" — enters at beat 5, before the recommendation, and shapes it: the register is not a wish list, it is a priced cull. Each option carries the Contrarian's test: signed AA cost-recovery or it doesn't make the register.

### Analogy / cross-domain pattern

A pre-closing punch list inverted in time: instead of listing defects after the building is done, list the decisions before they become defects. (Kept modest; the argument doesn't need more than this.)

### Signature visual

A two-axis exhibit: horizontal, the per-module fabrication-release timeline 2026–2029; vertical, decision categories from pour-locked to fully deferrable. Each Terminal F decision is a dot; dots left of "released" are gone. The visual makes one honest admission the run prompt didn't: some dots are already left of the line.

### The airport decision it leads toward

Board directs staff (with AA at the table) to adopt the reversibility register as a standing capital-governance artifact and to price the shortlisted options — Flagship-convertible shell, MARS minority, FIS envelope, Skylink node redundancy — before the next module tranche releases.

### Why it matters to the decision owner

McLaughlin is an operations-background CEO fifteen months into the job (record 144) presiding over $11.3B of concurrent CIP; the register converts an unbounded design debate into a bounded monthly agenda item he can run and the board can audit.

### Risk of choosing this frame

It is the safest and least memorable of the three. Eight agents already landed here; the report risks reading as a well-organized restatement of the swarm consensus. It also front-loads process over story, which the stated audience ("tired of McKinsey decks") may skim.

