## Option 2 — Counterintuitive: "American Is Telling You Terminal F Isn't Its Premium Terminal. Believe Them — Then Buy the Shell Anyway."

### Thesis (one sentence)

The strongest evidence in the record says American does not currently intend Terminal F as a premium flagship — and the correct response is not to override the tenant with premium bones it didn't ask for, but to encode the *generic* hub geometry that history shows outlives any carrier strategy, plus a short list of priced conversion options against the one certainty in the record: American will change its mind about something by 2043.

### Communication job

By the end, airport executives and capital planners should stop asking "how do we design for American's premium pivot" and start asking "how do we design for American reversing itself," because the ledger's most-corroborated single claim — Provisions by Admirals Club as Terminal F's announced lounge posture, twelve records across eight agents — is the tenant's own signal, and the six-terminal historical record shows carrier-specific buildings die with their carrier's strategy while generic ones absorb it.

### Opening scene

Not DFW. Detroit, February 24, 2002: the McNamara Terminal opens as Northwest's hub. Within seven years Northwest no longer exists — and the building absorbs the Delta merger without material retrofit, because it encoded a hub *form* (linear midfield, satellite concourses), not a Northwest form (`aviation-historian::ev-fe1739039376`). Cut to Pittsburgh, same building type, opposite outcome: $1B midfield terminal, US Airways de-hubs in 2004, the debt runs to 2019 (records 41, 174–177). Then the turn: DFW is now scoping a $4B, sole-occupancy terminal against one carrier's 2026 strategy, with a lease that runs to 2043.

### Argument sequence (seven beats)

1. **The premise under audit:** the run prompt says design for American's premium repositioning. Delta's Q4 2025 premium revenue crossing main cabin ($5.70B vs $5.62B) and AA's stated targets (narrowbody premium share 25%→40%, +50% lie-flat, 787-9P with 51 Flagship Suites) make the pivot real as a *fleet and revenue* story (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-ed425c651135`, records 190, 219).
2. **The tenant's revealed preference:** at DFW, American put its largest-ever Admirals Club (37,000 sf) in Terminal C, Flagship Check-In in Terminal D, and a grab-and-go Provisions concept in Terminal F (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-d063ac6892ae`, `contrarian::ev-e0e801801c25`; the ledger's most-corroborated claim). American simultaneously rebuilt the schedule from 9 to 13 daily banks — denser connecting pressure, not a premium-O&D flattening (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-e4842e2ba510`; and per §9, do not claim the rebank has demonstrably improved OTP — `operations-analyst::ev-6ad1dbdb3363` says DFW remains second-worst on departure rate).
3. **The follower problem:** AA is chasing Delta's premium economics with $34.7B of debt and a 0.2% 2025 net margin (records 60, 249), a decade-old $3B/$5B profit gap (record 222), and a 2020 precedent of closing every Flagship Lounge but JFK for 18 months (`contrarian::ev-02c3980fc7b3`). Even the leader delayed its own ATL mega-hub premium lounge to 2028 (`contrarian::ev-bbfb17e8f0b1`). Designing $4B of concrete around the follower's aspiration is the historical error pattern.
4. **The historical base rate:** six post-1978 purpose-built hub terminals; four failed with their carrier (PIT, STL, CVG, CLE — records 41–45, 174–183); the survivor encoded generic geometry (DTW). Sample of six, rhyme not law — and DFW's 82.6% share sits on a real two-city O&D economy PIT never had. Say so.
5. **The reframe:** therefore the design brief is not "premium terminal" — it is generic hub geometry (contiguous banks, MARS-capable minority of stands, FIS sized to the CBP standard, connecting-to-O&D flexibility across the contested 55–70% band per §4 Tension B) with carrier-specific ambition confined to fit-out, where it is reversible.
6. **The options list, Contrarian-disciplined:** the Flagship-convertible shell (risers, curtainwall breaks, structural allowance) at low marginal cost, versus the built lounge at $207–600M of displaced gate value (Quant Analyst); each option contingent on signed AA cost-recovery, because the industry norm puts premium fit-out on the carrier's side of the line (Delta One JFK T4, `contrarian::ev-c90731082056`).
7. **The 2035 falsification test (run-prompt area five):** this frame looks wrong in 2035 if AA has funded Flagship-tier space in F on its own balance sheet ahead of schedule, if connecting share has fallen materially below the 55–70% band (A321XLR-scale point-to-point erosion, Aviation Historian), or if CPE has breached the $20–22 behavioral threshold the Airline Strategist flags (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-9783f6e95c10`) and AA is contesting every optional dollar.

### Strongest objection and where it enters

Enters at beat 6, stated in its strongest form: the Contrarian says even the shell is a premium the tenant didn't order — buy two or three specific options on named gate positions with a signed AA cost-recovery MOU, refuse everything else. The narrative absorbs this as its pricing discipline rather than refuting it; the residual disagreement (shell everywhere vs. shell at named positions) is presented honestly as the report's live recommendation choice. A second objection — "you're arguing DFW is PIT, but 82.6% AA share on a top-six O&D market is nothing like Pittsburgh" — enters at beat 4 as a self-inflicted counter, which is where this frame earns its credibility.

### Analogy / cross-domain pattern

Spec-building versus build-to-suit in commercial real estate: the build-to-suit commands a premium until the named tenant's strategy changes, at which point the spec building is worth more. DTW was spec bones with build-to-suit finishes; PIT was build-to-suit to the studs. Genuinely clarifying here, because it maps exactly onto whose balance sheet carries premium fit-out.

### Signature visual

A six-terminal survival chart: PIT, STL, CVG, CLE, DTW, JFK T5 — opening cost, carrier event, and outcome (stranded / absorbed / repurposed) on one panel, with Terminal F's 2043 lease horizon drawn as a vertical line the reader can't unsee. Companion inset: American's own 2026 DFW premium map (37k sf club in C, Flagship Check-In in D, Provisions in F) — the tenant's signal, drawn rather than asserted.

### The airport decision it leads toward

Adopt "generic geometry, priced options, carrier-funded conversion" as the Terminal F design doctrine: lock MARS minority, FIS envelope, and Skylink redundancy into remaining fabrication releases; hold the Flagship shell decision open pending a written answer on whether the U&L obligates AA to any Flagship-tier presence in F before 2043 (evidence-map §8 — a contract question, not a design question).

### Why it matters to the decision owner

It gives the CEO and board a defensible public position for the one conversation they cannot avoid: telling their 82.6% tenant that the airport will buy conversion capacity but not the conversion — with the tenant's own announcements as the justification.

### Risk of choosing this frame

It can read as contradicting the run prompt's premise ("American is visibly repositioning toward premium") rather than sharpening it — the Strategist must hold both facts at once: the fleet-level pivot is real; the Terminal-F-level signal points the other way. Handled carelessly it becomes a "gotcha" against the operator's own framing. It also leans on a six-case historical sample the fact-checker will rightly force into hedged language.

