## Option 3 — Operational: "6:15 AM, Single Loop"

### Thesis (one sentence)

Judge every Terminal F design decision against one specific morning — the 6:15 AM connecting bank arriving while Skylink is still running one loop in one direction at 15-minute transit times — because that is the hour when the building's pour-locked geometry either carries American's 13-bank hub or becomes the reason the bank collapses.

### Communication job

By the end, capital planners and the COO's office should be able to name the four or five module-level decisions that determine whether Terminal F survives its worst ordinary morning — Skylink node redundancy, MARS stand minority, FIS queue depth, gate-hold sizing against bank contiguity — because the connecting bank, not the lounge, is the machine this building exists to serve.

### Opening scene

A composite morning, built strictly from ledger facts and labeled as a composite: 05:55, Skylink is still in overnight single-loop service — one direction, ~15-minute transit, as it is every night from 22:00 to 06:00 (`airport-coo::ev-9b8b1fc6f240`). The first of American's 13 daily banks is already inbound; under the rebanked schedule the Airline Strategist estimates ~52 departures in the 9 AM peak with bank contiguity replacing the old surge (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-e4842e2ba510`). Now replay the last week of January 2023 — ground stop, 4-hour average delays, 1,100+ cancellations day one (`airport-coo::ev-5bbd041de017`) — and ask which parts of Terminal F's geometry decide whether that morning propagates or dampens.

### Argument sequence (six beats)

1. **The machine:** Terminal F is a connecting building. Airport-level connecting share ~60%, run-prompt carrier-level claim ~two-thirds — contested, denominator named, both cited (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-d3646f7a1477`; §4 Tension B; the 55–70% band swings O&D throughput ~50%).
2. **The stress case:** the 13-bank restructure makes peaks denser, not flatter — and its benefits are not yet proven (departure rate +~1% YoY, still second-worst among busy US airports; block times lengthened +6 min on 145 markets, `operations-analyst::ev-6ad1dbdb3363`). Design to the unproven-benefit case, not the press release.
3. **Walk the morning through the building:** the Skylink node (platform edges, vertical circulation, queue depth — none confirmed in the public record, per §9; the recommendation is COO professional judgment and must be labeled as such); gate concurrency under bank contiguity; MARS stands as the pressure valve that lets one position work a widebody international arrival or two narrowbody bank turns (records 80–81, 203); FIS at CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth with 50–75 ft queue depth (`chief-engineer::ev-7dd287447303`) against AA's international shift to DFW (`airline-commercial-strategist::ev-6f157cc0ecd1`).
4. **Why this is urgent now:** every one of those is pour-locked, and the lock happens at release-for-fabrication — the August 2026 module move (`airport-coo::ev-f750431bb93f`) proves the decisions travel to site already made.
5. **The 18-month commissioning window (run-prompt area four):** Heathrow T5 opened on time and lost 42,000 bags in days (records 132–133, 248, `infrastructure-economist::ev-70c2c7cae06f`); LGA Terminal C delivered early under single-airline governance (`infrastructure-economist::ev-3ad44367f57d`). Terminal F's 2027 Phase 1 opens into a live 13-bank operation — the trial-period innovations (progressive activation, CUPPS-capable hardware as IROPS insurance per §4 Tension F, gate-camera conduit for turn analytics with vendor ROI treated as directional) belong to this window.
6. **The premium question, answered operationally:** premium at Terminal F is what survives the morning — reliable connections, a working Skylink node, an FIS that doesn't queue onto the sterile corridor. The Flagship shell rides along as a priced option, not the headline; Virtual Chris's arrivals-premium reframe gets one paragraph as the portfolio role AA hasn't covered.

### Strongest objection and where it enters

Enters at beat 2, from the COO's own chair: the COO reads the 13-bank restructure as a *flatter*, mid-day-loaded operation (§4 Tension D) — in which case the 6:15 AM stress case overstates the peak and the report is sizing to a ghost. The narrative answers with the Operations Analyst's data and the asymmetry argument (under-scoping the peak costs 2–4× in retrofit, presented as professional judgment per §9), but the tension is displayed, not dissolved. Second objection, from the Contrarian: an operational frame smuggles in the same option shopping list — so beat 5's innovations carry owners, cost orders of magnitude, and installation windows or they don't appear, exactly as the run prompt demands.

### Analogy / cross-domain pattern

A hospital designed around its emergency department's worst ordinary Saturday night rather than its lobby: the atrium is what visitors remember; the trauma-bay geometry is what determines outcomes. Used once, briefly — the airport reader doesn't need it belabored.

### Signature visual

A timeline-cutaway of Terminal F between 05:30 and 09:30: Skylink single-loop window shaded until 06:00, the first bank's arrivals overlaid, and the four pour-locked chokepoints (Skylink node, FIS queue, MARS positions, gate-hold) marked where the morning flows through them. One drawing that makes the argument walkable.

### The airport decision it leads toward

Direct the Terminal F program team to publish the designed capacity of the four chokepoints against the 13-bank morning (Skylink node throughput, FIS booths and queue depth, MARS count, gate-hold sizing at the contested connecting-share band) before the next fabrication release — and fund the deltas now, while they are factory-priced.

### Why it matters to the decision owner

This is the frame the COO and the CEO (an operations-background executive) can verify from their own floor: it converts a $4B design debate into four measurable throughput questions with dates attached.

### Risk of choosing this frame

The load-bearing scene rests partly on unconfirmed design facts — the Skylink node's platform-edge count and redundancy are not in the public record — so the narrative must repeatedly say "not confirmed as designed," which blunts the drama it is built on. It also under-serves run-prompt area one (the premium shift's physical implications), which becomes a supporting section rather than a spine, and the fact-checker will police the composite-morning device hard.

